WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:02.630
Let's talk about the economy a little bit.

00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:08.370
Um, Donald Trump loves to talk about the stock
market on days that the stock market goes

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.370
up.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:13.690
Sort of like today, looks like it's going
to be, but on days when the stock market is

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:19.599
down, like last Friday when the Dow Jones
industrial average was down 600 points, Trump

00:00:19.599 --> 00:00:21.310
says nothing about it.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:25.739
Sometimes on a down day, Trump will actually
say, you know, I don't really pay attention

00:00:25.739 --> 00:00:28.500
to the stock market, which is an obvious lie.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.630
It's very similar with GDP.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.689
GDP is gross domestic product.

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:36.510
It's another indicator that Donald Trump has
talked a lot about.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:43.100
He's predicted and promised 4% 5% even 6%
GDP growth.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.879
Those numbers were pretty crazy to begin with
and it's bearing out that Trump can't meet

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:47.879
them.

00:00:47.879 --> 00:00:56.910
And it turns out that fourth quarter GDP in
2019 went up 2.1% full year, 2019 GDP was

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.480
just 2.3%.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:01.860
That's the lowest in three years.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:09.250
Now, at a systemic level, we should be asking
questions like, what does GDP tell us about

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.049
an economy and what doesn't it tell us about
an economy?

00:01:12.049 --> 00:01:15.820
There's a lot that GDP doesn't tell us about
the state of the economy.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:21.250
What other indicators are important to understand
how the economy is doing for the average person,

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:25.950
just GDP doesn't tell us in the same way that
you know the stock market level doesn't tell

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:27.490
us you need other indicators.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:31.610
These are good important questions, but we
also have to talk about accountability and

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:34.040
holding politicians to what they say.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:38.409
Trump has been promising for years and the
white house has been predicting for years,

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:41.610
including for 2019 higher GDP numbers.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:43.210
They failed to get them.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:44.890
Now we can go further.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:50.820
One of the defenses of the 2017 tax reform
that Trump and Republicans passed was it might

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.740
look like it'll cut revenue to the government,
but it's going to be so stimulative to the

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:00.009
economy that GDP will go up, tax revenues
will go up.

00:02:00.009 --> 00:02:01.840
We're going to win hugely.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.509
None of that happened, but the super rich
still got their tax cuts, which we knew they

00:02:05.509 --> 00:02:06.650
were going to get.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:10.800
For the most part, Donald Trump ignores bad
GDP numbers and if you press him, he will

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:11.960
do what he's done his whole career.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.310
He'll blame somebody else lately.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:15.720
He blames the federal reserve.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.970
He says, you know, the federal reserve helped
Obama hugely with low federal funds, interest

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:20.970
rates.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.030
They're not helping me.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:25.520
The reality is the fed has kept rates quite
low.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.120
The fed has done some decreases to the federal
funds rate.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:33.750
Trump blaming the fed is another way to shift
the blame to someone, to anyone else.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.570
And by the way, who's in charge of the fed?

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:37.950
It's the guy that Donald Trump selected.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:42.750
So then remember when we look at GDP, we also
want to look at the deficit.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:47.410
And once again, because Donald Trump has put
so much stock in the deficit, he said he was

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:48.790
going to get us a surplus.

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:52.060
The surplus would pay off the national within
eight years.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:53.500
That's a joke.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.270
Some of his followers fell for it.

00:02:55.270 --> 00:03:00.840
The federal budget deficit is actually up
more than 20% from 2018 to 2019.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:06.590
So if we want to step back from the day to
day politics, one of the the relationships

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:12.620
we want to look at is how is GDP growth comparing
to deficit growth.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:17.970
Some so-called deficit Hawks always say if
the deficit is up, it's bad.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.980
They don't say it when a Republican is president,
but that's okay.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:23.660
They say it basically at all other times.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:29.010
Um, the reality though is if you want to have
a more sort of nuanced understanding of a

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:35.870
deficit, you want to look at how deficit is
changing in relation to GDP.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:40.610
They don't tend to care about that when it's
a democratic president.

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:41.790
Deficits bad.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:45.580
When it's a Republican president, we don't
talk about deficit deficit up.

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:46.890
That's bad debt up.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:47.890
That's bad.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:52.150
Unless it's a Republican president in which
we are in case we only talk about GDP numbers

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:53.150
or whatever.

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:54.980
So let me give you the non political analysis.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:57.190
Okay, I'm ignoring party.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:58.190
I don't care about party.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:02.710
I'm giving you a political and a non political
economic analysis.

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:06.880
As far as GDP growth goes, 2.3 is really not
bad.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:07.880
Okay.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:12.210
Just because Trump predicted more doesn't
mean 2.3% GDP growth is bad.

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:18.650
I would prefer 2.3 instead of these boom bust
cycles that are endemic to the type of economy

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:19.650
that we have.

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:21.069
2.3 is not bad.

00:04:21.069 --> 00:04:27.400
The real question is one that nobody who is
prominent in American politics in a position

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:32.689
of power right now is actually asking and
certainly not one that the media is giving

00:04:32.689 --> 00:04:33.689
attention.

00:04:33.689 --> 00:04:38.800
Uh, paying attention to which is, is this
sustainable to have an economy based on infinite

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:45.190
indefinite growth on a planet that at some
point is going to reach the limit of its resources?

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:50.889
And by the way, what are we going to do to
our environment if we actually strip the planet

00:04:50.889 --> 00:04:54.590
of all of those resources and burn them and
consume them and whatever else?

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:55.870
That's the real question.

00:04:55.870 --> 00:05:00.250
It's almost a completely ignored by both parties
and in the same way you know, the size of

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:01.250
the deficit.

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:03.219
It's a very amateurish thing to look at.

00:05:03.219 --> 00:05:05.930
The size of the deficit doesn't tell us that
much.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.720
The real question is why is the deficit going
up?

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:12.200
If it's going up, is the deficit going up
because of inflation?

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:16.540
Is the deficit going up because we're financing
healthcare and education, which are very good

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:20.539
for a lot of people and stimulative to the
economy or is the deficit going up to pay

00:05:20.539 --> 00:05:26.509
for the military industrial complex and you
know, foreign military entanglements and tax

00:05:26.509 --> 00:05:30.669
cuts for the rich which are stimulative to
the very few people that are getting the cash

00:05:30.669 --> 00:05:34.939
in their pockets but not by and large great
for the economy.

00:05:34.939 --> 00:05:40.120
These talking points on economic issues all
almost always fall completely short of really

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.860
informing anyone, not even about what the
answers are.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:45.469
They are asking the wrong questions.

00:05:45.469 --> 00:05:51.879
They are predicated on a limited, if not completely
wrong understanding of what it is that we

00:05:51.879 --> 00:05:53.509
should be paying attention to.

00:05:53.509 --> 00:05:55.529
The GDP stuff is no surprise.

00:05:55.529 --> 00:06:00.729
But of course, at the surface level, Donald
Trump making promises he simply cannot, cannot

00:06:00.729 --> 00:06:03.930
keep and which his followers will almost certainly
not hold him accountable for

